# Line endings.
#
# A shell script must be LF IN THE WORKING TREE, on every platform. git's Windows default is
# core.autocrlf=true, which checks them out CRLF -- and a CRLF shell script is not a cosmetic
# problem in this repository. Every one of these was observed here on a Windows checkout:
#
#   * bash on Linux refuses the file outright: `syntax error near unexpected token do'$'\r'`.
#   * a `perl -0pi -e 's/^...$/.../m'` injection in a negative control matches NOTHING, because
#     `$` does not sit before the CR. All sixteen plants of one control then reported "injection
#     did not land" -- the guard under test was never actually asked a question.
#   * a heredoc inside a CRLF script carries the CR into the embedded program's string literals,
#     so they cannot match a file the program reads in text mode.
#   * a trailing `\` line continuation followed by CR stops being a continuation.
#
# Pinning it here makes the repository state the requirement, instead of leaving it to each
# contributor's git configuration. Costs nothing where the checkout is already LF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The event loop under src/qb/ev/ is MIRRORED from isndev/qev, and dev/agent/check-qev-identity.py
# asserts the two copies are byte-identical. qev pins its whole tree to LF (`* text=auto eol=lf`);
# this repository pinned only `*.sh`, so on a Windows checkout qb's copy came out CRLF against
# qev's LF and all FOURTEEN mirrored files compared unequal -- a guard that could not pass, over
# content whose committed blobs are in fact identical (verified: 14 of 14 the same object id).
#
# Pinned here rather than relaxed in the guard. "Byte-identical" is the contract worth keeping,
# and the honest way to keep it is to make the two working trees actually identical rather than
# to teach the comparison to look away. Costs nothing on Linux and macOS: both were already LF.
src/qb/ev/** text eol=lf
